+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups, like `1h30m`. The units are `d` (days), `h`
+(hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once and in that order, without spaces. Units
+are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default or from Python
+is returned unchanged.
+
+Negative numbers, decimals, unknown units, repeated or out-of-order units, and spaces are rejected.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=30m1h'])
+```
+
